# =============================================================================
# Service Configuration
# =============================================================================
CERBERUS_GRPC_PORT=50051
CERBERUS_METRICS_PORT=9081
SIGNER_ENDPOINT=http://signer:50051

# =============================================================================
# Ingress Configuration
# =============================================================================
INGRESS=true
INGRESS_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0:8080
INGRESS_TIMEOUT_MS=0
# MAX_QUEUE_DEPTH: maximum number of tasks allowed in the ingress queue before the router sheds
# load. Once the queue is at capacity, POST /tasks returns 503 QUEUE_FULL with a Retry-After hint
# instead of accepting work that would be aggregated long after its block reference goes stale.
# The router processes one task at a time, so this bounds both memory and worst-case queue
# latency. Zero or unparseable values fall back to the default. Default: 100.
# MAX_QUEUE_DEPTH=100
# RATE_LIMIT_RPM: default per-API-key request rate on POST /tasks, in requests per minute.
# Applied to every key that was not issued with an explicit override (set at creation time via
# POST /admin/keys), so one client cannot monopolise the task queue. Requests over the limit get
# 429 with a Retry-After header. Counters are in-memory and reset on restart. Zero or unparseable
# values fall back to the default. Default: 60.
# RATE_LIMIT_RPM=60
# TASK_TTL_SECONDS: how long a task may sit without reaching a terminal state before a background
# sweep (every 60s) settles it as `expired`. Every task pins a block_height that goes stale as the
# chain advances, so a task the queue never reached in time can no longer yield a payload the
# contract accepts: it is expired with QUEUE_TTL_EXCEEDED rather than spending an aggregation round
# on a payload that would revert. A `ready` payload nobody collected is swept on the same TTL,
# counted from when aggregation recorded it, releasing the deduplication slot for its transition
# index (READY_TTL_EXCEEDED). A `processing` task is never cancelled mid-round. Expiries are
# counted in gas_killer_tasks_expired_total. Zero or unparseable values fall back to the default.
# The default is the wall-clock life of a rendered payload on L1 (PAYLOAD_BLOCK_BUFFER blocks x 12s
# slot time), so the sweep never withdraws a payload the chain would still accept; a shorter value
# suits a target chain with faster blocks, whose payload window is proportionally shorter.
# Default: 600.
# TASK_TTL_SECONDS=600
# RPC_FAILURE_THRESHOLD: how many consecutive RPC failures against one chain mark that chain
# unavailable. A chain that is down takes the whole task lifecycle with it — the ingress cannot
# validate a submission, the creator cannot analyse it, the executor cannot submit the resulting
# transaction — so once the threshold is reached the router publishes
# gas_killer_rpc_healthy{chain=...}=0, logs the transition at WARN, and refuses new submissions with
# 503 RPC_UNAVAILABLE rather than queueing work that cannot complete. Only L1 sheds traffic: every
# round anchors to an L1 reference block, so an L1 outage blocks submissions for L2 targets too,
# while a degraded L2 is alerted but leaves L1 targets alone. The count is a run, not a total: one
# success clears it. A background probe checks each chain every 15s, so the state tracks reality
# even while the router is idle and recovers on its own once the provider is back. Zero or
# unparseable values fall back to the default. Default: 5.
# RPC_FAILURE_THRESHOLD=5
# AVS_REFERENCE_TARGET / AVS_REFERENCE_TARGET_FILE / DEMO_TARGET_ADDRESS / DEMO_FACTORY_ADDRESS:
# feed the `contracts` block on GET /avs-metadata, which publishes the addresses an integrator must
# wire a target to in order to settle. A target pointed at a superseded signature checker passes
# every router-side check and then reverts InvalidQuorumApkHash on chain, so publishing the live
# pair is what makes that unwireable by accident.
#
# AVS_REFERENCE_TARGET is the deployed target whose avsAddress() and blsSignatureChecker() getters
# are read to establish that pair — authoritative by construction, since whatever a settling target
# verifies against is the answer. AVS_REFERENCE_TARGET_FILE names a file holding that address
# instead, which is what the Helm chart sets where the target deploy job runs: the job rewrites its
# record on every redeployment, so the pair tracks what was deployed with nothing to configure.
# Where neither is set, a demo_target.txt sitting beside avs_deploy.json is used, and failing that
# the block is left off. registryCoordinator comes from avs_deploy.json itself
# (AVS_DEPLOYMENT_PATH), and the checker's own registryCoordinator() is checked against it — a
# mismatch publishes nothing rather than a pair that reverts.
#
# DEMO_TARGET_ADDRESS and DEMO_FACTORY_ADDRESS are the contracts the documentation points readers
# at: a shared target for a first settlement with no Solidity written, and a factory for a
# caller-owned instance that concurrent readers cannot invalidate. Both are omitted from the
# response when unset, and each is checked for code on the chain the block publishes, so an address
# on another network is omitted rather than served under the wrong chainId. DEMO_TARGET_FILE and
# DEMO_FACTORY_FILE name files holding those addresses instead, which is what the Helm chart sets
# where the playground job runs — it records what it deployed, so the served addresses track a
# redeployment without anyone editing configuration. A named record the playground target is read
# from also becomes the preferred reference target, which keeps the published blsSignatureChecker
# equal to what demoTarget itself verifies against.
#
# Resolution runs off the request path — the endpoint is public and unauthenticated, so serving it
# must not turn into an RPC amplifier. Each attempt publishes the most complete set it can
# establish, and attempts repeat with backoff while a record a deploy job is expected to write is
# still unread: a provider blip does not leave the block absent until the next restart, a job that
# finishes late adds its addresses to what is already being served, and a job that fails outright
# does not withhold the addresses it has nothing to do with.
# AVS_REFERENCE_TARGET=0x...
# AVS_REFERENCE_TARGET_FILE=/app/.nodes/demo_target.txt
# DEMO_TARGET_ADDRESS=0x...
# DEMO_TARGET_FILE=/app/.nodes/playground_target.txt
# DEMO_FACTORY_ADDRESS=0x...
# DEMO_FACTORY_FILE=/app/.nodes/playground_factory.txt
# Shared secret guarding the /admin/keys endpoints, used to mint and revoke per-client API keys
# via `Authorization: Bearer <ADMIN_KEY>`. Clients then authenticate POST /tasks with their minted
# key. Leave unset to disable the admin API. Required for TESTNET mode with ingress enabled.
# ADMIN_KEY=

# =============================================================================
# Persistence
# =============================================================================
# Directory for the router's SQLite database (router.db lives here). Created on startup if
# absent; must be writable by the process. In Kubernetes this is a PVC-backed mount.
DATA_DIR=/data

# =============================================================================
# AVS Metadata (served at GET /avs-metadata; set this URL as metadata.uri in config.json)
# =============================================================================
# AVS_METADATA_NAME=Gas Killer
# AVS_METADATA_WEBSITE=https://gaskiller.xyz
# AVS_METADATA_DESCRIPTION=Verifiable off-chain compute service for EVM smart contracts via EigenLayer
# AVS_METADATA_LOGO=https://...
# AVS_METADATA_TWITTER=https://x.com/...
#
# Operator set entry (omit AVS_OPSET_NAME to exclude the operatorSets field entirely)
# AVS_OPSET_NAME=Gas Analysis Quorum
# AVS_OPSET_ID=0
# AVS_OPSET_DESCRIPTION=BLS-aggregated operator set for verifiable gas analysis tasks
# AVS_OPSET_SOFTWARE_NAME=gas-killer-node
# AVS_OPSET_SOFTWARE_DESCRIPTION=Receives tasks, runs EVM gas analysis, returns a BLS-signed result
# AVS_OPSET_SOFTWARE_URL=https://github.com/gas-killer/service
# AVS_OPSET_SLASHING_CONDITIONS=Signing a conflicting result for the same task ID

# =============================================================================
# Gas Killer Trigger Inputs (optional for E2E test)
# =============================================================================
GAS_KILLER_ROUTER_URL=http://localhost:8080
GAS_KILLER_API_KEY=gk_
# GAS_KILLER_TARGET_ADDRESS= # Will be set automatically to "ArraySummation" if deployed by script
# GAS_KILLER_CALL_DATA= # 0x-prefixed hex (function selector + params)
# GAS_KILLER_FROM_ADDRESS= # 0x-prefixed hex
# GAS_KILLER_TRANSITION_INDEX=1
# GAS_KILLER_VALUE=0
# GAS_KILLER_STORAGE_UPDATES= # 0x-prefixed hex

# =============================================================================
# Schnorr operator set (SIGNATURE_SCHEME=schnorr only)
# =============================================================================
# Registering the operator set is a separate phase from deploying a target, run by the
# `setup_schnorr_operators` binary. It MUST complete first: every registration advances the
# registry's effectiveBlock watermark, and verification fail-closes for reference blocks
# behind it. The binary is a no-op under bls, so callers run it unconditionally.
#
# Helm deployments run this phase in-cluster as the chart's `schnorr-operators` job, between the
# setup and deploy-target jobs. The variables below cover a bare-metal or compose run; the
# chart's equivalents live under `schnorr` in helm/gas-killer/values.yaml.
#
# Where the operators' secp256k1 key files live. Defaults to an `operator_keys` directory
# beside AVS_DEPLOYMENT_PATH, which is where the eigenlayer setup container writes them.
# OPERATOR_KEYS_DIR=config/.nodes/operator_keys
#
# Reuse an already-populated registry instead of deploying a fresh one and re-registering.
# Its operator set is assumed complete; the binary fails if the address has no code.
# SCHNORR_STAKE_REGISTRY_ADDRESS=

# =============================================================================
# Example Contracts Harness (scripts/examples/)
# =============================================================================
# The public example-contracts library is fetched and built by
# scripts/examples/fetch_examples.sh, then deployed by the `deploy_example` bin, which
# reads the manifest at scripts/examples/examples.toml. See scripts/README.md.
#
# Where the checkout and its forge artifacts live.
# EXAMPLES_DIR=.examples/example-contracts
# Git revision to build. Pinned to a SHA so a rebuild is reproducible; override with a
# branch name to track upstream.
# EXAMPLES_REF=f9aa0a6020ec7b3e28db47999ece8ba54b3847f6
# EXAMPLES_REPO=https://github.com/gas-killer/example-contracts
#
# Constructor wiring for deployed examples. Both default to reading the AVS deployment
# JSON at AVS_DEPLOYMENT_PATH, which is what the local compose stack produces; set them
# explicitly when deploying against a testnet with no local deployment JSON.
# EXAMPLE_AVS_ADDRESS=          # default: addresses.avsServiceManagerWrapper
# EXAMPLE_SIG_CHECKER_ADDRESS=  # default: addresses.IncredibleSquaringTaskManager
#
# NOTE: the signature checker must be a real BLSSignatureChecker (one exposing
# registryCoordinator()). addresses.blsSigCheck is the BLSSigCheckOperatorStateRetriever,
# which has no checkSignatures — a target wired to it reverts with an empty 0x during
# verifyAndUpdate. deploy_example rejects it rather than letting that happen silently.
